WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.779
INTRO:
Have you ever heard of a company called TSMC?

00:00:03.779 --> 00:00:09.150
While most people have never heard about TSMC,
all of us are extremely dependent on the company.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:21.390
As of July 2020, TSMC controlled 51% of the
entire global chip market.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:31.630
And when you look at consumer electronics,
the numbers are just even higher.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:39.219
In terms of iPhones, supercomputers, and automotive
AI chips, TSMC is responsible for 92% of production

00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:42.149
while Samsung controls the remaining 8%.

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:45.899
Considering their monopoly on the semiconductor
market, I don’t think you’d be surprised

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:51.030
to hear that they’re the 10th largest company
in the world coming in at $607 billion.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.660
Yet, the general public has never heard about
TSMC.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:00.370
So, here’s how TSMC became the king of semiconductor
manufacturing.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:06.240
MORRIS CHANG:
Taking a look back, the story of TSMC dates

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.390
back nearly a hundred years to a man named
Morris Chang who was born on July 10, 1931

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:12.549
in Ningbo, Chekiang.

00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:17.649
A lot of Morris’ formative years were during
the height of World War 2, so his family found

00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:21.100
themselves constantly moving around China
to avoid areas of conflict.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.330
Initially, they moved from Ningbo to British
Hong Kong, and this is where Morris would

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:26.600
attend primary school.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.119
But, by the time he was 11, Japan would invade
and overtake Hong Kong which forced the family

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:36.049
to move to Shanghai, then back to Ningbo,
and then to Chongqing.

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:40.600
This lasted for a couple of years, but in
1948, the Chinese Civil War would break out,

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.280
and the family would move to Hong Kong once
again.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.040
Fortunately for Morris though, he was about
to turn 18 which meant that he could attend

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.299
college in a much more stable area.

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:53.520
Morris initially wanted to be a writer or
journalist, but his father convinced him that

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.590
engineering was a much more stable path.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:02:00.020
And with that, Morris would move to the US
and attend Harvard University.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.649
He only stuck around at Harvard for one year
though as he would transfer to MIT during

00:02:03.649 --> 00:02:05.079
his second year.

00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:07.950
Morris would get his bachelors in mechanical
engineering in 1952, and he would instantly

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.420
turn around and get his masters as well in
1953.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:17.570
Morris also pursued a doctoral degree from
MIT, but he would eventually quit in 1955

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.540
after his thesis was rejected multiple times.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.180
Given that Morris already had a bachelors
and masters in engineering though, it wasn’t

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.010
that hard for him to find a job.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:31.300
In 1955, Morris joined Sylvania Semiconductor
and he would work there for 3 years before

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:35.110
switching over to Texas Instruments, and this
is where he would spend the majority of his

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.720
working days.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.010
Unlike many entrepreneurs who start a business
during college or after a couple of years

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.570
of work, Morris held a job till it was basically
time to retire.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:46.840
He slowly worked his way up the corporate
ladder and stayed at Texas Instruments for

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.400
25 years.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.220
In 1961, Texas Instruments sponsored Morris’
PhD in electrical engineering from Stanford,

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.150
and I think this is what really got Morris
to stick around.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.340
After receiving his PhD, Morris got promoted
to Vice President and eventually President

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.340
of TI.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.890
It wasn’t until 1983 that Morris left TI
when he got offered a job as COO at General

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:08.960
Instrument Corporation.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.880
He only stuck around for a year though before
he quit in 1985.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:17.010
At this point, Morris was 55 years old, and
I’m sure he made plenty of money as an engineer

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.010
and executive.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:22.540
So, he could’ve easily retired, but instead
he decided to partner with Taiwan and Philips

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.380
and founded TSMC in 1987.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:30.730
BREAKING IN:
By the time TSMC was created though, legacy

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:34.760
players like Intel, TI, and NEC dominated
the market.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:38.800
And given that it took years to develop and
manufacture advanced semiconductors, the barrier

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.060
to enter the industry was quite high.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:45.180
But, on the brightside, if you were able to
cross that barrier, you had a great shot as

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.400
brand value doesn’t mean much in this industry.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.120
After all, consumers don’t know or care
about where these chips come from, so electronics

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.130
companies just choose the most cost effective
supplier.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:04:01.320
TSMC’s first chips were 2 process nodes
or basically 2 generations behind the competition,

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.930
but Morris was able to make this work by pricing
their semiconductors ahead of the cost curve.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.601
In other words, Morris took a loss at the
beginning in order to attract customers, and

00:04:09.601 --> 00:04:12.440
he leveraged economies of scale to turn a
profit.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:17.340
While simple in theory, this idea was extremely
controversial in the 1980s as it was extremely

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.609
difficult to reach said economies of scale.

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:24.000
So, Morris was making a massive gamble with
this strategy, but given that he was backed

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.870
by Taiwan and Philips, he had some pretty
deep pockets.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:32.060
Within 4 to 5 years, Morris was able to reduce
the gap between TSMC and the legacy players

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.000
to just 1 process node.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.240
And around the same time, Morris would take
TSMC public in 1993 through the Taiwan Stock

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:39.240
Exchange.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.270
4 years later, TSMC would become the first
Taiwanese company to be listed on the New

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:44.970
York Stock Exchange.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:48.570
With the help of the public markets and the
irrational dotcom bubble, Morris was able

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:52.790
to continue his super aggressive strategy
throughout the 1990s, and TSMC would catch

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:56.340
up to the legacy players by the end of the
century.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:01.560
GLOBAL GIANT:
TSMC had finally broken into the semiconductor

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.970
industry, and they could’ve taken it easy
by simply keeping up with the competition.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:07.580
But, Morris had no plans of slowing down.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:11.850
Thus far, he had dedicated the company’s
efforts into catching up to the competition,

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:16.190
and now he was going to pour all of their
effort into becoming the global leader.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:20.220
Given that he was over 70 years old though,
Morris would hand over the position of CEO

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:21.430
to Rick Tsai.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.970
Under Rick’s leadership, TSMC continued
to rapidly innovate their chips.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:29.900
In fact, between 2006 and 2008, Rick oversaw
the company’s biggest leap in manufacturing

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:32.870
from 65 nanometer chips to 40 nanometer chips.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:37.530
But, despite this fast progress, TSMC didn’t
grow all that much during this time period

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:42.360
as the global chip industry was experiencing
a massive recession following the dotcom bubble.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.650
And it didn’t take long for the 2008 financial
recession to roll around, but Morris actually

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.010
saw great opportunity through this recession.

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:53.490
He witnessed the emergence of mobile technology
like smartphones and tablets, and he was extremely

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:55.800
excited to be part of this revolution.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.490
In fact, Morris was so excited and he saw
so much opportunity that he would return as

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.190
CEO in 2009 at age 78.

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:07.060
It looks like Morris' primary goal with returning
to TSMC was to make a deal with Apple.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:11.240
And in 2011, this would become a reality as
Apple agreed to begin trial production of

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:14.080
the A5 and A6 chips with TSMC.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:17.860
From there, it didn’t take long for Apple
to commit to working with TSMC for the long

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:18.860
term.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:22.480
And today, Apple accounts for 25% of TSMC’s
entire revenue.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:26.570
Anyway, after the Apple partnership was solidified,
Morris announced that he was going to retire

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:28.650
in 2013 and make Rick CEO again.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:33.460
But, when 2013 actually came around, Morris
decided to stick around even longer.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:37.610
Not only was the mobile space exploding, but
Nvidia and AMD were about to go on their own

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:38.980
exponential runs.

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:43.120
Nvidia started embracing the machine learning
and AI industries in the mid 2010s as the

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.330
PC market started to stagnate.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:49.180
In the meantime, AMD was basically employing
Morris’ strategy of pricing chips ahead

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:55.020
of the cost curve with Ryzen, and this turned
out to be a massive success.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:59.620
To make things even better, Bitcoin exploded
in 2017 which resulted in crazy high demand

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:03.210
for Nvidia and AMD GPUs for crypto mining
rigs.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:08.090
And given that TSMC is the supplier for Nvidia,
AMD, Apple, and everyone else you can think

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.920
of, this boom directly carried over to TSMC.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:16.169
TSMC managed to break their dotcom highs in
late 2016, and profits started to grow exponentially

00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:17.310
once again.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:22.050
And with that, Morris would finally retire
in June of 2018 at age 86.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:26.640
SUPPLY SHORTAGE:
After Morris left, Mark Liu who served as

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.639
co CEO became CEO and Chairman.

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:33.800
Over the next few years, TSMC started to stagnate
once again.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.530
And though this makes Mark look bad, it’s
really not his fault.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.950
Morris is just really good at leaving at the
peak.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:44.389
Anyway, though the company stagnated for a
few years, once the pandemic and supply shortage

00:07:44.389 --> 00:07:46.979
hit, TSMC would go parabolic once again.

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:50.760
However, the supply shortage has not been
all good for TSMC.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.400
Sure, their profits and stock price have gone
to the moon, but the shortage has also drawn

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:58.560
a lot of attention towards TSMC’s monopoly.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.419
Basically all of the chip shortages circle
back to TSMC, and it’s not particularly

00:08:02.419 --> 00:08:05.130
a good thing that one company has so much
influence.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:09.090
So, many governments around the world have
been trying to diversify chip production.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:13.710
For example, former president Donald Trump
pressured TSMC into building a $12 billion

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.100
manufacturing plant in Phoenix.

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:19.729
Similarly, TSMC agreed to open a research
and development facility in Japan.

00:08:19.729 --> 00:08:24.110
In the meantime, the European Union is trying
to beat TSMC to 2 nm production.

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:26.840
But, this is going to be extremely difficult.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:31.810
TSMC themselves are only on 3 nm production,
and they’ve never slowed their innovation

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.020
momentum.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:37.199
According to Peter Hanbury, if you want to
compete against TSMC on 3 nm, you’re going

00:08:37.199 --> 00:08:41.719
to have to spend $15 billion this year, then
$18 billion next year, and then $20 billion

00:08:41.719 --> 00:08:43.209
the year after.

00:08:43.209 --> 00:08:48.019
And that’s just 3 nms, the European Union
wants to beat TSMC to 2 nms.

00:08:48.019 --> 00:08:51.870
The problem is that as technology continues
to improve, it becomes exponentially harder

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:54.260
and more expensive to take the next step.

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:58.180
So, it’s basically impossible to just step
in and compete against TSMC.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:03.410
You would likely need a budget of $100 billion
to beat TSMC to 2 nm in a short period of

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:07.379
time, and though the EU can technically afford
that, it’s unlikely that they’ll actually

00:09:07.379 --> 00:09:09.120
make such a large investment.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:13.410
As more time goes by though, it’ll just
become even harder to challenge TSMC though,

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:18.639
so maybe the EU will bite the bullet, but
we’ll just have to wait and see.

00:09:18.639 --> 00:09:23.790
ETHICAL MONOPOLY:
While TSMC is no doubt a monopoly, it could

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:26.740
be argued that TSMC has thus far been an ethical
monopoly.

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:31.029
They haven’t voluntarily jacked up prices
or slowed down innovation or been involved

00:09:31.029 --> 00:09:34.279
in any sort of corruption even though all
of these would’ve been super easy given

00:09:34.279 --> 00:09:36.110
their influence and control.

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:40.199
TSMC is actually extremely strict about ethics
and integrity.

00:09:40.199 --> 00:09:44.259
Just a few months ago, they fired seven employees
for leaking information about Apple’s upcoming

00:09:44.259 --> 00:09:45.259
chips.

00:09:45.259 --> 00:09:49.029
Since Morris founded TSMC, the company has
centered around 4 core values which include

00:09:49.029 --> 00:09:52.920
integrity, commitment, innovation, and customer
trust.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.439
While these sound super cliche, TSMC’s history
over the past 34 years has shown that TSMC

00:09:57.439 --> 00:09:59.360
has indeed upheld these values.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:09.499
I mean just take a look at how Nvidia’s
CEO Jensen Huang talks about TSMC.

00:10:09.499 --> 00:10:31.139
Hearing that, I think it’s pretty clear
that the world’s biggest companies don’t

00:10:31.139 --> 00:10:34.990
work with TSMC because they have to, but because
they want to.

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:39.360
And as long as that’s the case, maybe the
TSMC monopoly isn’t all that bad.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:42.390
Do you guys think that something has to be
done about TSMC?

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:43.819
Comment that down below.

